{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated report-reviewer worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Review only scientific validity and inference scope. Return {\"findings\":[{\"check_id\":\"CITE-CONTEXT|SCOPE-INFLATE|ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK|RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\"severity\":\"info|minor|major|critical\",\"evidence\":[{\"id\":str,\"locator\":str,\"sha256\":str}],\"recommendation\":str}],\"summary\":str}. Use major only when the direction result cannot be admitted without repair.\nINPUT:\n{\"question\": \"Immune homeostasis is a state of internal equilibrium. Without homeostasis, the immune system cannot function correctly, leading to disease and infection. Knowing how this balance is maintained can provide a fuller picture of how the body works and may help us in calibrating medical interventions. For instance, instead of nonselectively boosting or suppressing the whole immune system as we currently do, in the future we may be able to rebalance a dysregulated immune system. Studies in humans and in animal models have pursued almost every aspect of immune response and examined many immune cell populations, yet several unanswered questions remain. Genomic, proteomic, and other ‘omics data are helpful. But the human immune system is dynamic and very individualized, making animal models of limited use—thus a robust platform to study and manipulate human immune cells is needed to address the problem.\", \"direction\": {\"title\": \"Human immune-cell perturbation platform\", \"rationale\": \"Establishes a high-fidelity ex vivo and organoid-based experimental framework to directly manipulate human immune networks, bypassing species-specific translational gaps inherent in animal models.\", \"workflow\": \"wet_lab_proposal\", \"completion_test\": \"A validated standard operating procedure for multi-parameter single-cell readouts post-perturbation, complete with dose-response matrices, viability controls, and predefined success thresholds for signaling fidelity.\", \"remaining\": \"IRB clearance, patient sample procurement, and bench-scale protocol validation.\", \"position\": 0}, \"evidence\": {\"sources\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\", \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\", \"title\": \"Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"content\": \"## Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC\\n\\n**Source**: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Skip to main content](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/#main-content)\\n\\n**Official websites use .gov**\\nA**.gov**website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\\n\\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\\nA**lock**() or**https://**means you've safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\\n\\n[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\\n\\n- [Journal List](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/)\\n- [User Guide](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/userguide/)\\n\\n-\\n- [](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\\n- [](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/pdf/nihms304953.pdf)\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n\\n## PERMALINK\\n\\nAs a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM\\nor the National Institutes of Health.\\nLearn more:[PMC Disclaimer](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/disclaimer/)|[PMC Copyright Notice](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/copyright/)\\n\\n. Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2012 Feb 7.\\n\\n*Published in final edited form as:*Science. 2011 May 6;332(6030):687–696. doi:[10.1126/science.1198704](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\\n\\n# Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum\\n\\n[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Bendall%20SC\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Sean C Bendall\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Bendall%20SC\\\"[Author])1,*,[Erin F\\nSimonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Simonds%20EF\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Erin F Simonds\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Erin F Simonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Simonds%20EF\\\"[Author])1,*,[Peng\\nQiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Qiu%20P\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Peng Qiu\\n\\n2Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Peng Qiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Qiu%20P\\\"[Author])2,[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Amir%20EAD\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### El-ad D Amir\\n\\n3Department of Biological Sciences, Columbia University, New York, NY 10027, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Amir%20EAD\\\"[Author])3,[Peter O\\nKrutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Krutzik%20PO\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Peter O Krutzik\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Peter O Krutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Krutzik%20PO\\\"[Author])1,[Rachel\\nFinck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Finck%20R\\\"[Author])\\n\\n### Rachel Finck\\n\\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\\n\\nFind articles by[Rachel Finck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Finck%20R\\\"[Author])1,[Robert V\\nBruggner](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\\\"Bruggner%20RV\\\"[Author])\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"title\": \"Immune Cell Modulation of Patient-Matched Organoid Drug Response in Precision Cancer Medicine Platform\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells15030259\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"content\": \"extract_upstream_error\\nTarget returned an unsupported response.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\", \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\", \"title\": \"Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"content\": \"## Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d69e5d8a-9808-4353-a607-9c8e9f37a64e#content)\\n\\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\\nand JavaScript.\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nAntibodies are produced when naive B cells differentiate into plasma cells within germinal centres (GCs) of lymphoid tissues. Patients with B cell lymphoma on\\neffective immunotherapies exhibit diminished antibody production, leading to higher infection rates and reduced vaccine efficacy, even after B cell recovery.\\nCurrent ex vivo models fail to sustain long-term GC reactions and effectively test B cell responses. Here we developed synthetic hydrogels mimicking the\\nlymphoid tissue microenvironment, enabling human GCs from tonsils and peripheral blood mononuclear cell-derived B cells. Immune organoids derived from\\nperipheral blood mononuclear cells maintain GC B cells and plasma cells longer than tonsil-derived ones and exhibit unique B cell programming, including GC\\ncompartments, somatic hypermutation, immunoglobulin class switching and B cell clones. Chemical inhibition of transcriptional and epigenetic processes enhances\\nplasma cell formation. While integrating polarized CXCL12 protein in a lymphoid organ-on-chip modulates GC responses in healthy donor B cells, it fails with B\\ncells derived from patients with lymphoma. Our system allows rapid, controlled modelling of immune responses and B cell disorders.\\n\\nThis is a preview of subscription content,[access via your\\ninstitution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\\n\\n## Access options\\n\\n[Access through your institution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\\n\\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\\n\\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\\n\\n$32.99/ 30 days\\n\\ncancel any time\\n\\n[Learn more](https://shop-amers.nature.com/products/plus/?region=US)\\n\\nSubscribe to this journal\\n\\nReceive 12 print issues and online access\\n\\n$259.00 per year\\n\\nonly $21.58 per issue\\n\\n[Learn more](https://www.nature.com/nmat/subscribe)\\n\\nBuy this article\\n- Purchase on SpringerLink\\n- Instant access to the full article PDF.\\n\\nUSD 39.95\\n\\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\\n\\n**Fig. 1: Bioengineered hydrogel-based tonsil-derived lymphoid organoids.**\\n\\n**Fig. 2: PBMC-derived hydrogel-based organoids capture donor variability and adjuvant response.**\\n\\n**Fig. 3: Transcriptomic, BCR, clonal and SHM changes in human lymphoid organoids.**\\n\\n**Fig. 4: Simultaneous suppression of EZH2 and BCL6 in GC B cells enhances plasma cell generation.**\\n\\n**Fig. 5: Anti-CD20 immunotherapy impairs the GC formation capacity of reconstituted B cells in patients with lymphoma.**\\n\\n**Fig. 6: Lymphocyte-encapsulated tissues with switchable gradients in organs-on-chip.**\\n\\n### Similar content being viewed by others\\n\\n### [Regeneration of immunocompetent B lymphopoiesis from pluripotent stem cells guided by transcription\\nfactors](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41423-021-00805-6?fromPaywallRec=true)\\n\\nArticleOpen access10 December 2021\\n\\n### [Recreation of an antigen-driven germinal center in vitro by providing B cells with phagocytic\\nantigen](https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-023-04807-0?fromPaywallRec=true)\\n\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\", \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\", \"title\": \"Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\", \"url\": \"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"content\": \"## Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ORIGINAL RESEARCH article\\n\\nFront. Immunol., 23 July 2025\\n\\nSec. T Cell Biology\\n\\nVolume 16 - 2025 |[https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707](https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707)\\n\\n# *Ex vivo-*generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\\n\\n- [\\n\\nPG\\n\\nPierre Gaudeaux1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1839749)\\n- [\\n\\nJP\\n\\nJuliette Paillet1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2954479)\\n- [\\n\\nMA\\n\\nMonah Abou Alezz3](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3053927)\\n- [\\n\\nRD\\n\\nRanjita Devi Moirangthem1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2955460)\\n-\\n\\nSC\\n\\nSara Cascione3\\n- [\\n\\nMM\\n\\nMarta Martin Corredera1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2876570)\\n- [\\n\\nAD\\n\\nAnne-Catherine Dolens4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1122973)\\n-\\n\\nKD\\n\\nKatrien De Mulder4,5\\n- [\\n\\nIV\\n\\nImke Velghe4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3075834)\\n- [\\n\\nBV\\n\\nBart Vandekerckhove4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/387046)\\n- [\\n\\nML\\n\\nMarieke Lavaert4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/965927)\\n- [\\n\\nNR\\n\\nNoémie Robil6](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2198787)\\n- [\\n\\nAC\\n\"}], \"summaries\": {\"summaries\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"summary\": \"Metadata page for a 2011 Science publication by Bendall et al. detailing single-cell mass cytometry analysis of differential immune and drug responses across the human hematopoietic continuum.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-525; scope limited to publication metadata, author affiliations, and journal navigation due to preview formatting.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"summary\": \"Source retrieval failed; content consists solely of an extraction error indicating an unsupported response from the target server.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-2; no substantive scientific data available for summarization.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"summary\": \"Introduces synthetic hydrogel-based immune organoids that replicate lymphoid microenvironments to sustain long-term germinal center reactions. Demonstrates that peripheral blood-derived organoids outperform tonsil-derived ones in maintaining B cell programming and plasma cell generation, which can be enhanced via transcriptional/epigenetic inhibition. Highlights differential responses to immunotherapies and microenvironmental cues between healthy donors and lymphoma patients, establishing a controlled platform for modeling B cell biology and disorders.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-704; scope restricted to abstract and figure captions due to subscription preview status.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"summary\": \"Metadata page for a 2025 Frontiers in Immunology research article investigating ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors and their dual differentiation potential into T cells and innate lymphoid cells.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-580; scope limited to publication header, author list, and journal navigation elements.\"}]}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions.\"}], \"summary\": \"The proposed human immune-cell perturbation platform relies on fragmented, inaccessible, or erroneous source material. Available evidence consists primarily of publication metadata, a single extraction failure, and a paywalled abstract focusing narrowly on B-cell organoids. Consequently, the rationale overstates the breadth of applicable data, and the specified completion test (validated SOP with dose-response matrices and signaling thresholds) is entirely unsupported. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow and adjust the scope accordingly.\"}}, \"claims\": {\"claims\": [{\"node_id\": \"node:177ed12d69b18d71697ec7720cdf6f6a\", \"text\": \"In synthetic hydrogel-based lymphoid organoids, peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC)-derived constructs will demonstrate statistically significant prolongation of germinal center B-cell and plasma-cell maintenance compared to tonsil-derived equivalents under identical culture conditions.\", \"kind\": \"prediction\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\"], \"scope\": \"Comparative longevity analysis of PBMC versus tonsil-derived B-cell lymphoid organoids in standardized hydrogel matrices\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 0, \"challenge\": \"The claim overlooks substantial donor-to-donor immunological variability and ex vivo expansion artifacts that can artificially prolong cell survival independent of tissue origin, while lacking the required viability controls and statistical power to substantiate 'statistically significant' differences.\", \"repair\": \"Restrict scope to donor-matched comparative cohorts within a single hydrogel batch, implement rigorous viability normalization against fresh primary isolates, and mandate pre-study power calculations alongside standardized survival curves before asserting significance.\"}}, {\"node_id\": \"node:130b7378ba0085b3b2ac628b75205964\", \"text\": \"Anti-CD20 immunotherapy in lymphoid organ-on-chip systems imposes a hard functional bound on germinal center formation capacity in lymphoma patient-derived B cells, preventing rescue via polarized CXCL12 microenvironmental gradients.\", \"kind\": \"bound\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\"], \"scope\": \"Therapeutic intervention limits on lymphoma patient B-cell germinal center dynamics within gradient-modulated organ-on-chip platforms\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 1, \"challenge\": \"Characterizing the anti-CD20 effect as a 'hard functional bound' overstates the mechanism, as therapeutic depletion may spare transitional/memory subsets or allow residual niche retention; additionally, the claim omits critical missing observations regarding anti-CD20 dosing kinetics and CXCL12 gradient concentration thresholds needed to prove absolute non-rescue.\", \"repair\": \"Reframe the assertion as a conditional suppression threshold dependent on specific drug concentrations and gradient intensities, incorporate healthy donor B-cell baselines to isolate disease-specific impairment, and define explicit rescue failure criteria based on quantitative GC architecture metrics.\"}}, {\"node_id\": \"node:02a59a61ec4bf1b7fe80814c00178524\", \"text\": \"Concurrent chemical inhibition of EZH2 and BCL6 transcriptional regulators in PBMC-derived hydrogel organoids will produce a quantifiable increase in terminal plasma cell differentiation rates exceeding single-target epigenetic blockade thresholds.\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\"], \"scope\": \"Epigenetic and transcriptional co-inhibition efficacy in enhancing plasma cell generation within human B-cell organoid models\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 2, \"challenge\": \"Concurrent EZH2 and BCL6 inhibition introduces high risk of off-target cytotoxicity and aberrant lineage commitment that could confound differentiation readouts, while the claim ignores missing observations on transcriptional feedback loops and lacks single-agent dose-response baselines required to mathematically verify synergistic exceedance of thresholds.\", \"repair\": \"Integrate viability-corrected lineage tracing assays, standardize inhibitor administration windows relative to germinal center induction, and apply formal synergy quantification models (e.g., Bliss independence or Chou-Talalay) using rigorously titrated single-target controls to validate combined efficacy.\"}}], \"rebuttal\": {\"rebuttals\": [{\"claim_index\": 0, \"challenge\": \"The claim overlooks substantial donor-to-donor immunological variability and ex vivo expansion artifacts that can artificially prolong cell survival independent of tissue origin, while lacking the required viability controls and statistical power to substantiate 'statistically significant' differences.\", \"repair\": \"Restrict scope to donor-matched comparative cohorts within a single hydrogel batch, implement rigorous viability normalization against fresh primary isolates, and mandate pre-study power calculations alongside standardized survival curves before asserting significance.\"}, {\"claim_index\": 1, \"challenge\": \"Characterizing the anti-CD20 effect as a 'hard functional bound' overstates the mechanism, as therapeutic depletion may spare transitional/memory subsets or allow residual niche retention; additionally, the claim omits critical missing observations regarding anti-CD20 dosing kinetics and CXCL12 gradient concentration thresholds needed to prove absolute non-rescue.\", \"repair\": \"Reframe the assertion as a conditional suppression threshold dependent on specific drug concentrations and gradient intensities, incorporate healthy donor B-cell baselines to isolate disease-specific impairment, and define explicit rescue failure criteria based on quantitative GC architecture metrics.\"}, {\"claim_index\": 2, \"challenge\": \"Concurrent EZH2 and BCL6 inhibition introduces high risk of off-target cytotoxicity and aberrant lineage commitment that could confound differentiation readouts, while the claim ignores missing observations on transcriptional feedback loops and lacks single-agent dose-response baselines required to mathematically verify synergistic exceedance of thresholds.\", \"repair\": \"Integrate viability-corrected lineage tracing assays, standardize inhibitor administration windows relative to germinal center induction, and apply formal synergy quantification models (e.g., Bliss independence or Chou-Talalay) using rigorously titrated single-target controls to validate combined efficacy.\"}]}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions.\"}], \"summary\": \"The proposed platform's rationale overstates its breadth relative to the narrow, largely inaccessible evidence base, which consists primarily of metadata, a paywalled abstract focused on B-cell organoids, and one extraction failure. Furthermore, the completion test mandates validated SOPs, dose-response matrices, and signaling thresholds that are entirely absent from the provided materials. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow, and the scope must be narrowed to match the available B-cell-centric data before the direction can be admitted.\"}}, \"experiment\": {\"protocol\": {\"claim_index\": 2, \"hypothesis\": \"Concurrent chemical inhibition of EZH2 and BCL6 in PBMC-derived hydrogel organoids will yield a statistically significant, viability-corrected increase in terminal plasma cell differentiation rates that mathematically exceeds the additive thresholds predicted by single-target epigenetic blockade alone.\", \"samples\": \"Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) isolated from healthy adult donors, expanded ex vivo, and embedded in standardized synthetic PEG-fibrinogen hydrogels to form B-cell lymphoid organoids under defined germinal center induction conditions.\", \"perturbations\": [\"EZH2-specific small molecule inhibitor (titrated dose-response)\", \"BCL6-specific small molecule inhibitor (titrated dose-response)\", \"Concurrent dual inhibition at matched molar ratios across the dose matrix\"], \"controls\": [\"Vehicle control (DMSO equivalent)\", \"Single-agent EZH2 inhibition at identical concentrations\", \"Single-agent BCL6 inhibition at identical concentrations\", \"Fresh primary PBMC baseline (day 0 viability and phenotype)\"], \"endpoints\": [\"Terminal plasma cell frequency (CD19- CD27high CD38high) via flow cytometry\", \"Germinal center architectural integrity (area/volume fraction)\", \"Cell viability and proliferation index (CFSE/Ki-67)\", \"Transcriptional output (PRDM1, XBP1, IRF4 mRNA/protein levels)\", \"Phospho-signaling readouts for downstream BCR/TGF-beta pathways\"], \"analysis_plan\": \"Generate full dose-response matrices for all perturbations. Normalize all differentiation endpoints to day-0 fresh isolates and correct for compound-induced cytotoxicity. Calculate synergy indices using Bliss Independence and Chou-Talalay models. Compare dual-inhibition outcomes against single-agent curves using two-way ANOVA with Bonferroni correction. Apply pre-study power calculations to confirm statistical significance before finalizing conclusions.\", \"stopping_rules\": [\"Terminate experiment if overall viability drops below 60% at any timepoint\", \"Abort dose escalation if IC50 shifts indicate non-specific membrane disruption\", \"Cease cohort expansion if donor-matched variability exceeds predefined CV thresholds (>15%)\", \"Stop if no measurable GC structure forms by day 7 regardless of treatment\"], \"manual_result_schema\": {\"donor_id\": \"string\", \"hydrogel_batch_id\": \"string\", \"treatment_condition\": \"string\", \"concentration_nM\": \"number\", \"days_in_culture\": \"integer\", \"viability_percent\": \"number\", \"plasma_cell_frequency_percent\": \"number\", \"gc_architecture_score\": \"number\", \"bliss_synergy_index\": \"number\", \"chou_talalay_ci\": \"number\", \"prdm1_fold_change\": \"number\"}, \"safety_and_ethics\": [\"IRB-approved informed consent for human peripheral blood collection\", \"Adherence to BSL-2 laboratory practices for human cell culture\", \"Proper handling and disposal of cytotoxic small-molecule inhibitors per institutional EHS guidelines\", \"De-identification of all donor samples and compliance with HIPAA/GDPR data protection standards\"], \"cannot_establish_without_execution\": \"Computational environments and dry-lab simulations cannot replicate the complex biophysical constraints of hydrogel matrices, dynamic cytokine gradients, or real-time epigenetic-transcriptional crosstalk required to validate synergistic plasma cell differentiation; empirical wet-lab execution with multi-parameter single-cell readouts is strictly necessary.\"}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions.\"}], \"summary\": \"The proposed platform's rationale overstates its breadth relative to the narrow, largely inaccessible evidence base, which consists primarily of metadata, a paywalled abstract focused on B-cell organoids, and one extraction failure. Furthermore, the completion test mandates validated SOPs, dose-response matrices, and signaling thresholds that are entirely absent from the provided materials. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow, and the scope must be narrowed to match the available B-cell-centric data before the direction can be admitted.\"}, \"observations\": null}, \"brief\": {\"title\": \"Human Immune-Cell Perturbation Platform Handoff: B-Cell Organoid & Epigenetic Modulation Focus\", \"learned\": [\"Synthetic hydrogel-based lymphoid organoids can successfully sustain germinal center reactions and model human B-cell programming.\", \"PBMC-derived constructs demonstrate potential longevity advantages over tonsil-derived equivalents for maintaining plasma cell populations.\", \"Concurrent chemical inhibition of EZH2 and BCL6 is hypothesized to synergistically drive terminal plasma cell differentiation beyond single-target blockade.\", \"The current evidence base is fragmented, consisting primarily of publication metadata, paywalled abstracts, and extraction failures rather than validated experimental protocols.\"], \"evidence\": [\"Qualitatively supports the use of engineered hydrogels to mimic lymphoid microenvironments for controlled B-cell response modeling.\", \"Demonstrates differential therapeutic responses (e.g., anti-CD20 impairment) and microenvironmental sensitivity between healthy donors and lymphoma patients.\", \"Validates the technical feasibility of multi-parameter mass cytometry and transcriptomic profiling to track hematopoietic continuum dynamics post-perturbation.\", \"Indicates that targeted epigenetic/transcriptional modulation can influence germinal center architecture and plasma cell output in vitro.\"], \"limitations\": [\"Cannot substantiate comprehensive immune network manipulation due to a narrow focus on B-cells and absence of T-cell/innate lymphoid data.\", \"Lacks the validated standard operating procedures, dose-response matrices, viability controls, and predefined signaling fidelity thresholds mandated by the completion test.\", \"Fails to account for donor-to-donor immunological variability, ex vivo expansion artifacts, and potential off-target cytotoxicity without empirical wet-lab execution.\", \"Dry-lab simulations and computational models cannot replicate the complex biophysical constraints of hydrogel matrices or real-time epigenetic-transcriptional crosstalk.\"], \"open_questions\": [\"What are the precise molar ratios and dosing kinetics for concurrent EZH2/BCL6 inhibition that maximize synergy while maintaining >60% cell viability?\", \"How does inter-donor variability impact organoid reproducibility, and what statistical power metrics are required to normalize survival curves?\", \"Can polarized CXCL12 gradients or other microenvironmental cues overcome anti-CD20-induced suppression in lymphoma-derived B-cell niches?\", \"Which specific multi-parameter readouts and viability-correction algorithms best define 'signaling fidelity' across heterogeneous primary isolates?\"], \"next_moves\": [\"Secure IRB approval and procure matched healthy/lymphoma donor peripheral blood mononuclear cells for cohort expansion.\", \"Develop and internally validate the wet-lab SOP, establishing titrated dose-response matrices, vehicle controls, and standardized viability normalization protocols.\", \"Execute bench-scale hydrogel organoid cultures with EZH2/BCL6 perturbations, capturing multi-parameter flow cytometry and transcriptomic endpoints.\", \"Apply formal synergy quantification models (Bliss Independence, Chou-Talalay) and pre-study power calculations to generate definitive success thresholds.\", \"Temporarily narrow the directional scope to B-cell lymphoid organoids pending acquisition of broader immune network validation data.\"]}}"}]}
{"type": "model_output", "text": "{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\"}\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\"}\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 525}, \"sha256\": \"70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 704}, \"sha256\": \"f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785\"},\n        {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 580}, \"sha256\": \"75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf\"}\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The proposed human immune-cell perturbation platform relies on fragmented, inaccessible, or erroneous source material. Available evidence consists primarily of publication metadata, a single extraction failure, and a paywalled abstract focusing narrowly on B-cell organoids. Consequently, the rationale overstates the breadth of applicable data, and the specified completion test (validated SOP with dose-response matrices and signaling thresholds) is entirely unsupported. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow and adjust the scope accordingly.\"\n}", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 13334, "total_tokens": 20865, "completion_tokens": 7531, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 5532, "text_tokens": 7531}}, "model": "qwen3.7-flash"}
{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"question": "Immune homeostasis is a state of internal equilibrium. Without homeostasis, the immune system cannot function correctly, leading to disease and infection. Knowing how this balance is maintained can provide a fuller picture of how the body works and may help us in calibrating medical interventions. For instance, instead of nonselectively boosting or suppressing the whole immune system as we currently do, in the future we may be able to rebalance a dysregulated immune system. Studies in humans and in animal models have pursued almost every aspect of immune response and examined many immune cell populations, yet several unanswered questions remain. Genomic, proteomic, and other ‘omics data are helpful. But the human immune system is dynamic and very individualized, making animal models of limited use—thus a robust platform to study and manipulate human immune cells is needed to address the problem.", "direction": {"title": "Human immune-cell perturbation platform", "rationale": "Establishes a high-fidelity ex vivo and organoid-based experimental framework to directly manipulate human immune networks, bypassing species-specific translational gaps inherent in animal models.", "workflow": "wet_lab_proposal", "completion_test": "A validated standard operating procedure for multi-parameter single-cell readouts post-perturbation, complete with dose-response matrices, viability controls, and predefined success thresholds for signaling fidelity.", "remaining": "IRB clearance, patient sample procurement, and bench-scale protocol validation.", "position": 0}, "evidence": {"sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "artifact_id": "artifact:70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3", "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3", "title": "Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "content": "## Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum - PMC\n\n**Source**: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/\n\n---\n\n[Skip to main content](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/#main-content)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\nA**.gov**website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\nA**lock**() or**https://**means you've safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\n\n- [Journal List](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/)\n- [User Guide](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/userguide/)\n\n-\n- [](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\n- [](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/pdf/nihms304953.pdf)\n-\n-\n-\n\n## PERMALINK\n\nAs a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM\nor the National Institutes of Health.\nLearn more:[PMC Disclaimer](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/disclaimer/)|[PMC Copyright Notice](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/copyright/)\n\n. Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2012 Feb 7.\n\n*Published in final edited form as:*Science. 2011 May 6;332(6030):687–696. doi:[10.1126/science.1198704](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198704)\n\n# Single-Cell Mass Cytometry of Differential Immune and Drug Responses Across a Human Hematopoietic Continuum\n\n[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Bendall%20SC\"[Author])\n\n### Sean C Bendall\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Sean C Bendall](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Bendall%20SC\"[Author])1,*,[Erin F\nSimonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Simonds%20EF\"[Author])\n\n### Erin F Simonds\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Erin F Simonds](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Simonds%20EF\"[Author])1,*,[Peng\nQiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Qiu%20P\"[Author])\n\n### Peng Qiu\n\n2Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Peng Qiu](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Qiu%20P\"[Author])2,[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Amir%20EAD\"[Author])\n\n### El-ad D Amir\n\n3Department of Biological Sciences, Columbia University, New York, NY 10027, USA\n\nFind articles by[El-ad D Amir](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Amir%20EAD\"[Author])3,[Peter O\nKrutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Krutzik%20PO\"[Author])\n\n### Peter O Krutzik\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Peter O Krutzik](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Krutzik%20PO\"[Author])1,[Rachel\nFinck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Finck%20R\"[Author])\n\n### Rachel Finck\n\n1Baxter Laboratory in Stem Cell Biology, Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA\n\nFind articles by[Rachel Finck](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Finck%20R\"[Author])1,[Robert V\nBruggner](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Bruggner%20RV\"[Author])"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "artifact_id": "artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "title": "Immune Cell Modulation of Patient-Matched Organoid Drug Response in Precision Cancer Medicine Platform", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3390/cells15030259", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "artifact_id": "artifact:f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785", "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785", "title": "Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "content": "## Human immune organoids to decode B cell response in healthy donors and patients with lymphoma | Nature Materials\n\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1\n\n---\n\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41563-024-02037-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d69e5d8a-9808-4353-a607-9c8e9f37a64e#content)\n\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\nand JavaScript.\n\n## Abstract\n\nAntibodies are produced when naive B cells differentiate into plasma cells within germinal centres (GCs) of lymphoid tissues. Patients with B cell lymphoma on\neffective immunotherapies exhibit diminished antibody production, leading to higher infection rates and reduced vaccine efficacy, even after B cell recovery.\nCurrent ex vivo models fail to sustain long-term GC reactions and effectively test B cell responses. Here we developed synthetic hydrogels mimicking the\nlymphoid tissue microenvironment, enabling human GCs from tonsils and peripheral blood mononuclear cell-derived B cells. Immune organoids derived from\nperipheral blood mononuclear cells maintain GC B cells and plasma cells longer than tonsil-derived ones and exhibit unique B cell programming, including GC\ncompartments, somatic hypermutation, immunoglobulin class switching and B cell clones. Chemical inhibition of transcriptional and epigenetic processes enhances\nplasma cell formation. While integrating polarized CXCL12 protein in a lymphoid organ-on-chip modulates GC responses in healthy donor B cells, it fails with B\ncells derived from patients with lymphoma. Our system allows rapid, controlled modelling of immune responses and B cell disorders.\n\nThis is a preview of subscription content,[access via your\ninstitution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\n\n## Access options\n\n[Access through your institution](https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41563-024-02037-1)\n\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\n\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n\n$32.99/ 30 days\n\ncancel any time\n\n[Learn more](https://shop-amers.nature.com/products/plus/?region=US)\n\nSubscribe to this journal\n\nReceive 12 print issues and online access\n\n$259.00 per year\n\nonly $21.58 per issue\n\n[Learn more](https://www.nature.com/nmat/subscribe)\n\nBuy this article\n- Purchase on SpringerLink\n- Instant access to the full article PDF.\n\nUSD 39.95\n\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\n\n**Fig. 1: Bioengineered hydrogel-based tonsil-derived lymphoid organoids.**\n\n**Fig. 2: PBMC-derived hydrogel-based organoids capture donor variability and adjuvant response.**\n\n**Fig. 3: Transcriptomic, BCR, clonal and SHM changes in human lymphoid organoids.**\n\n**Fig. 4: Simultaneous suppression of EZH2 and BCL6 in GC B cells enhances plasma cell generation.**\n\n**Fig. 5: Anti-CD20 immunotherapy impairs the GC formation capacity of reconstituted B cells in patients with lymphoma.**\n\n**Fig. 6: Lymphocyte-encapsulated tissues with switchable gradients in organs-on-chip.**\n\n### Similar content being viewed by others\n\n### [Regeneration of immunocompetent B lymphopoiesis from pluripotent stem cells guided by transcription\nfactors](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41423-021-00805-6?fromPaywallRec=true)\n\nArticleOpen access10 December 2021\n\n### [Recreation of an antigen-driven germinal center in vitro by providing B cells with phagocytic\nantigen](https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-023-04807-0?fromPaywallRec=true)\n"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "artifact_id": "artifact:75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf", "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5c465aaf", "title": "Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "content": "## Frontiers | Ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\n\n**Source**: https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707/full\n\n---\n\n## ORIGINAL RESEARCH article\n\nFront. Immunol., 23 July 2025\n\nSec. T Cell Biology\n\nVolume 16 - 2025 |[https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707](https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1617707)\n\n# *Ex vivo-*generated lymphoid progenitors encompass both T cell and innate lymphoid cell fates\n\n- [\n\nPG\n\nPierre Gaudeaux1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1839749)\n- [\n\nJP\n\nJuliette Paillet1,2†](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2954479)\n- [\n\nMA\n\nMonah Abou Alezz3](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3053927)\n- [\n\nRD\n\nRanjita Devi Moirangthem1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2955460)\n-\n\nSC\n\nSara Cascione3\n- [\n\nMM\n\nMarta Martin Corredera1,2](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2876570)\n- [\n\nAD\n\nAnne-Catherine Dolens4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/1122973)\n-\n\nKD\n\nKatrien De Mulder4,5\n- [\n\nIV\n\nImke Velghe4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3075834)\n- [\n\nBV\n\nBart Vandekerckhove4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/387046)\n- [\n\nML\n\nMarieke Lavaert4,5](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/965927)\n- [\n\nNR\n\nNoémie Robil6](https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/2198787)\n- [\n\nAC\n"}], "summaries": {"summaries": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "summary": "Metadata page for a 2011 Science publication by Bendall et al. detailing single-cell mass cytometry analysis of differential immune and drug responses across the human hematopoietic continuum.", "limits": "Lines 1-525; scope limited to publication metadata, author affiliations, and journal navigation due to preview formatting."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "summary": "Source retrieval failed; content consists solely of an extraction error indicating an unsupported response from the target server.", "limits": "Lines 1-2; no substantive scientific data available for summarization."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "summary": "Introduces synthetic hydrogel-based immune organoids that replicate lymphoid microenvironments to sustain long-term germinal center reactions. Demonstrates that peripheral blood-derived organoids outperform tonsil-derived ones in maintaining B cell programming and plasma cell generation, which can be enhanced via transcriptional/epigenetic inhibition. Highlights differential responses to immunotherapies and microenvironmental cues between healthy donors and lymphoma patients, establishing a controlled platform for modeling B cell biology and disorders.", "limits": "Lines 1-704; scope restricted to abstract and figure captions due to subscription preview status."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "summary": "Metadata page for a 2025 Frontiers in Immunology research article investigating ex vivo-generated lymphoid progenitors and their dual differentiation potential into T cells and innate lymphoid cells.", "limits": "Lines 1-580; scope limited to publication header, author list, and journal navigation elements."}]}, "review": {"findings": [{"check_id": "CITE-CONTEXT", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework."}, {"check_id": "SCOPE-INFLATE", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation."}, {"check_id": "RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions."}], "summary": "The proposed human immune-cell perturbation platform relies on fragmented, inaccessible, or erroneous source material. Available evidence consists primarily of publication metadata, a single extraction failure, and a paywalled abstract focusing narrowly on B-cell organoids. Consequently, the rationale overstates the breadth of applicable data, and the specified completion test (validated SOP with dose-response matrices and signaling thresholds) is entirely unsupported. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow and adjust the scope accordingly."}}, "claims": {"claims": [{"node_id": "node:177ed12d69b18d71697ec7720cdf6f6a", "text": "In synthetic hydrogel-based lymphoid organoids, peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC)-derived constructs will demonstrate statistically significant prolongation of germinal center B-cell and plasma-cell maintenance compared to tonsil-derived equivalents under identical culture conditions.", "kind": "prediction", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a"], "scope": "Comparative longevity analysis of PBMC versus tonsil-derived B-cell lymphoid organoids in standardized hydrogel matrices", "rebuttal": {"claim_index": 0, "challenge": "The claim overlooks substantial donor-to-donor immunological variability and ex vivo expansion artifacts that can artificially prolong cell survival independent of tissue origin, while lacking the required viability controls and statistical power to substantiate 'statistically significant' differences.", "repair": "Restrict scope to donor-matched comparative cohorts within a single hydrogel batch, implement rigorous viability normalization against fresh primary isolates, and mandate pre-study power calculations alongside standardized survival curves before asserting significance."}}, {"node_id": "node:130b7378ba0085b3b2ac628b75205964", "text": "Anti-CD20 immunotherapy in lymphoid organ-on-chip systems imposes a hard functional bound on germinal center formation capacity in lymphoma patient-derived B cells, preventing rescue via polarized CXCL12 microenvironmental gradients.", "kind": "bound", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a"], "scope": "Therapeutic intervention limits on lymphoma patient B-cell germinal center dynamics within gradient-modulated organ-on-chip platforms", "rebuttal": {"claim_index": 1, "challenge": "Characterizing the anti-CD20 effect as a 'hard functional bound' overstates the mechanism, as therapeutic depletion may spare transitional/memory subsets or allow residual niche retention; additionally, the claim omits critical missing observations regarding anti-CD20 dosing kinetics and CXCL12 gradient concentration thresholds needed to prove absolute non-rescue.", "repair": "Reframe the assertion as a conditional suppression threshold dependent on specific drug concentrations and gradient intensities, incorporate healthy donor B-cell baselines to isolate disease-specific impairment, and define explicit rescue failure criteria based on quantitative GC architecture metrics."}}, {"node_id": "node:02a59a61ec4bf1b7fe80814c00178524", "text": "Concurrent chemical inhibition of EZH2 and BCL6 transcriptional regulators in PBMC-derived hydrogel organoids will produce a quantifiable increase in terminal plasma cell differentiation rates exceeding single-target epigenetic blockade thresholds.", "kind": "hypothesis", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a"], "scope": "Epigenetic and transcriptional co-inhibition efficacy in enhancing plasma cell generation within human B-cell organoid models", "rebuttal": {"claim_index": 2, "challenge": "Concurrent EZH2 and BCL6 inhibition introduces high risk of off-target cytotoxicity and aberrant lineage commitment that could confound differentiation readouts, while the claim ignores missing observations on transcriptional feedback loops and lacks single-agent dose-response baselines required to mathematically verify synergistic exceedance of thresholds.", "repair": "Integrate viability-corrected lineage tracing assays, standardize inhibitor administration windows relative to germinal center induction, and apply formal synergy quantification models (e.g., Bliss independence or Chou-Talalay) using rigorously titrated single-target controls to validate combined efficacy."}}], "rebuttal": {"rebuttals": [{"claim_index": 0, "challenge": "The claim overlooks substantial donor-to-donor immunological variability and ex vivo expansion artifacts that can artificially prolong cell survival independent of tissue origin, while lacking the required viability controls and statistical power to substantiate 'statistically significant' differences.", "repair": "Restrict scope to donor-matched comparative cohorts within a single hydrogel batch, implement rigorous viability normalization against fresh primary isolates, and mandate pre-study power calculations alongside standardized survival curves before asserting significance."}, {"claim_index": 1, "challenge": "Characterizing the anti-CD20 effect as a 'hard functional bound' overstates the mechanism, as therapeutic depletion may spare transitional/memory subsets or allow residual niche retention; additionally, the claim omits critical missing observations regarding anti-CD20 dosing kinetics and CXCL12 gradient concentration thresholds needed to prove absolute non-rescue.", "repair": "Reframe the assertion as a conditional suppression threshold dependent on specific drug concentrations and gradient intensities, incorporate healthy donor B-cell baselines to isolate disease-specific impairment, and define explicit rescue failure criteria based on quantitative GC architecture metrics."}, {"claim_index": 2, "challenge": "Concurrent EZH2 and BCL6 inhibition introduces high risk of off-target cytotoxicity and aberrant lineage commitment that could confound differentiation readouts, while the claim ignores missing observations on transcriptional feedback loops and lacks single-agent dose-response baselines required to mathematically verify synergistic exceedance of thresholds.", "repair": "Integrate viability-corrected lineage tracing assays, standardize inhibitor administration windows relative to germinal center induction, and apply formal synergy quantification models (e.g., Bliss independence or Chou-Talalay) using rigorously titrated single-target controls to validate combined efficacy."}]}, "review": {"findings": [{"check_id": "CITE-CONTEXT", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework."}, {"check_id": "SCOPE-INFLATE", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation."}, {"check_id": "RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions."}], "summary": "The proposed platform's rationale overstates its breadth relative to the narrow, largely inaccessible evidence base, which consists primarily of metadata, a paywalled abstract focused on B-cell organoids, and one extraction failure. Furthermore, the completion test mandates validated SOPs, dose-response matrices, and signaling thresholds that are entirely absent from the provided materials. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow, and the scope must be narrowed to match the available B-cell-centric data before the direction can be admitted."}}, "experiment": {"protocol": {"claim_index": 2, "hypothesis": "Concurrent chemical inhibition of EZH2 and BCL6 in PBMC-derived hydrogel organoids will yield a statistically significant, viability-corrected increase in terminal plasma cell differentiation rates that mathematically exceeds the additive thresholds predicted by single-target epigenetic blockade alone.", "samples": "Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) isolated from healthy adult donors, expanded ex vivo, and embedded in standardized synthetic PEG-fibrinogen hydrogels to form B-cell lymphoid organoids under defined germinal center induction conditions.", "perturbations": ["EZH2-specific small molecule inhibitor (titrated dose-response)", "BCL6-specific small molecule inhibitor (titrated dose-response)", "Concurrent dual inhibition at matched molar ratios across the dose matrix"], "controls": ["Vehicle control (DMSO equivalent)", "Single-agent EZH2 inhibition at identical concentrations", "Single-agent BCL6 inhibition at identical concentrations", "Fresh primary PBMC baseline (day 0 viability and phenotype)"], "endpoints": ["Terminal plasma cell frequency (CD19- CD27high CD38high) via flow cytometry", "Germinal center architectural integrity (area/volume fraction)", "Cell viability and proliferation index (CFSE/Ki-67)", "Transcriptional output (PRDM1, XBP1, IRF4 mRNA/protein levels)", "Phospho-signaling readouts for downstream BCR/TGF-beta pathways"], "analysis_plan": "Generate full dose-response matrices for all perturbations. Normalize all differentiation endpoints to day-0 fresh isolates and correct for compound-induced cytotoxicity. Calculate synergy indices using Bliss Independence and Chou-Talalay models. Compare dual-inhibition outcomes against single-agent curves using two-way ANOVA with Bonferroni correction. Apply pre-study power calculations to confirm statistical significance before finalizing conclusions.", "stopping_rules": ["Terminate experiment if overall viability drops below 60% at any timepoint", "Abort dose escalation if IC50 shifts indicate non-specific membrane disruption", "Cease cohort expansion if donor-matched variability exceeds predefined CV thresholds (>15%)", "Stop if no measurable GC structure forms by day 7 regardless of treatment"], "manual_result_schema": {"donor_id": "string", "hydrogel_batch_id": "string", "treatment_condition": "string", "concentration_nM": "number", "days_in_culture": "integer", "viability_percent": "number", "plasma_cell_frequency_percent": "number", "gc_architecture_score": "number", "bliss_synergy_index": "number", "chou_talalay_ci": "number", "prdm1_fold_change": "number"}, "safety_and_ethics": ["IRB-approved informed consent for human peripheral blood collection", "Adherence to BSL-2 laboratory practices for human cell culture", "Proper handling and disposal of cytotoxic small-molecule inhibitors per institutional EHS guidelines", "De-identification of all donor samples and compliance with HIPAA/GDPR data protection standards"], "cannot_establish_without_execution": "Computational environments and dry-lab simulations cannot replicate the complex biophysical constraints of hydrogel matrices, dynamic cytokine gradients, or real-time epigenetic-transcriptional crosstalk required to validate synergistic plasma cell differentiation; empirical wet-lab execution with multi-parameter single-cell readouts is strictly necessary."}, "review": {"findings": [{"check_id": "CITE-CONTEXT", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Retrieve full-text versions or substitute with accessible primary literature detailing wet-lab SOPs, dose-response validation, and multi-parameter single-cell readout protocols to substantiate the proposed framework."}, {"check_id": "SCOPE-INFLATE", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Narrow the directional scope to align with available evidence (e.g., B-cell lymphoid organoids and mass cytometry profiling) or acquire broader immunological perturbation data before claiming comprehensive immune network manipulation."}, {"check_id": "RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e7d9528c8ec67265fdf076e446fd7cee359584dba60e41868edae3d418927d4a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 525}, "sha256": "70d20034e09f95f1c3cea9f4e5ecc43004fe3373bf32d1f6ff7503dfff2334c3"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:4d9edaa447a746aa39ddc3a645e81517c7b755fe46dcdec3c9c9db6bae3d6816", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:9fe214bb4a026b0310437b977b53d875eeadf12eea2401c60fa54ee50ff5e39a", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 704}, "sha256": "f8046f04a867c75a6c3a4809a52dc55bd000d157d1bfdaf79846b84811932785"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:08861704d4587675cc21542e4702b386a05f9ec022534d0dc4e7bfc9a568731d", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 580}, "sha256": "75e1b7dfc452d67e98ba1a00c26ae20f009951bdaf815ea385eeffda5465aaf"}], "recommendation": "Develop and document the required SOP, dose-response matrices, and viability controls internally prior to submission, as current sources provide zero procedural validation or threshold definitions."}], "summary": "The proposed platform's rationale overstates its breadth relative to the narrow, largely inaccessible evidence base, which consists primarily of metadata, a paywalled abstract focused on B-cell organoids, and one extraction failure. Furthermore, the completion test mandates validated SOPs, dose-response matrices, and signaling thresholds that are entirely absent from the provided materials. Substantive full-text literature or internal experimental documentation is required to validate the workflow, and the scope must be narrowed to match the available B-cell-centric data before the direction can be admitted."}, "observations": null}, "brief": {"title": "Human Immune-Cell Perturbation Platform Handoff: B-Cell Organoid & Epigenetic Modulation Focus", "learned": ["Synthetic hydrogel-based lymphoid organoids can successfully sustain germinal center reactions and model human B-cell programming.", "PBMC-derived constructs demonstrate potential longevity advantages over tonsil-derived equivalents for maintaining plasma cell populations.", "Concurrent chemical inhibition of EZH2 and BCL6 is hypothesized to synergistically drive terminal plasma cell differentiation beyond single-target blockade.", "The current evidence base is fragmented, consisting primarily of publication metadata, paywalled abstracts, and extraction failures rather than validated experimental protocols."], "evidence": ["Qualitatively supports the use of engineered hydrogels to mimic lymphoid microenvironments for controlled B-cell response modeling.", "Demonstrates differential therapeutic responses (e.g., anti-CD20 impairment) and microenvironmental sensitivity between healthy donors and lymphoma patients.", "Validates the technical feasibility of multi-parameter mass cytometry and transcriptomic profiling to track hematopoietic continuum dynamics post-perturbation.", "Indicates that targeted epigenetic/transcriptional modulation can influence germinal center architecture and plasma cell output in vitro."], "limitations": ["Cannot substantiate comprehensive immune network manipulation due to a narrow focus on B-cells and absence of T-cell/innate lymphoid data.", "Lacks the validated standard operating procedures, dose-response matrices, viability controls, and predefined signaling fidelity thresholds mandated by the completion test.", "Fails to account for donor-to-donor immunological variability, ex vivo expansion artifacts, and potential off-target cytotoxicity without empirical wet-lab execution.", "Dry-lab simulations and computational models cannot replicate the complex biophysical constraints of hydrogel matrices or real-time epigenetic-transcriptional crosstalk."], "open_questions": ["What are the precise molar ratios and dosing kinetics for concurrent EZH2/BCL6 inhibition that maximize synergy while maintaining >60% cell viability?", "How does inter-donor variability impact organoid reproducibility, and what statistical power metrics are required to normalize survival curves?", "Can polarized CXCL12 gradients or other microenvironmental cues overcome anti-CD20-induced suppression in lymphoma-derived B-cell niches?", "Which specific multi-parameter readouts and viability-correction algorithms best define 'signaling fidelity' across heterogeneous primary isolates?"], "next_moves": ["Secure IRB approval and procure matched healthy/lymphoma donor peripheral blood mononuclear cells for cohort expansion.", "Develop and internally validate the wet-lab SOP, establishing titrated dose-response matrices, vehicle controls, and standardized viability normalization protocols.", "Execute bench-scale hydrogel organoid cultures with EZH2/BCL6 perturbations, capturing multi-parameter flow cytometry and transcriptomic endpoints.", "Apply formal synergy quantification models (Bliss Independence, Chou-Talalay) and pre-study power calculations to generate definitive success thresholds.", "Temporarily narrow the directional scope to B-cell lymphoid organoids pending acquisition of broader immune network validation data."]}}}}
